Attractive occasional trips, in several forms depending on offer content, train type and infrastructure (modern or old/historic):
• tours of natural attractions;
• tours of cultural & historical heritage;
• tours of various events.
They may use modern infrastructure OR valorise old, low-speed railways. High-speed trains also suit one-day excursions (city-centre stations).

• DOMINANT position in serving intercontinental passenger & tourist flows;
• needs NO infrastructure along the route — only the start/end points (AIRPORTS); routes are 'imagined' corridors;
• great SPEED (usually up to ~900 km/h), surpassing all other modes;
• covers very LONG distances, making even remote parts of the world accessible (a driver of globalisation).

• INELASTICITY — dependence on start/end points; needs ANOTHER mode (usually road) to/from the airport;
• airports occupy large areas FAR from the city → transfer time can exceed the flight;
• HIGH fuel consumption (defying gravity);
• HIGH aircraft purchase price & operating costs;
• relatively MODEST capacity (~500 passengers on average).

No. Most air traffic is by airplanes, but other carriers exist:
• BALLOONS — far more economical than airplanes but much less safe (now used for freight, telecom, advertising, tourism);
• ZEPPELINS (rigid airships) — historically used for transoceanic transport (e.g. the Hindenburg, abandoned after the 1937 disaster); still exist in modern forms.

Typical quality dimensions (confirmed by the EU rail & coach surveys):
• SAFETY & feeling of safety;
• PUNCTUALITY & reliability;
• PRICE/fare (value for money);
• COMFORT & cleanliness;
• FREQUENCY & availability;
• INFORMATION (timetables, real-time, delays);
• ease of buying tickets & integrated/through-ticketing;
• ACCESSIBILITY (incl. reduced-mobility assistance) & complaint handling.

Quality is deliberately TRADED against price & purpose:
• CHARTER — lower quality (less comfort, worse times/airports) for a lower price & high occupancy;
• SCHEDULED/traditional — higher quality, reliability and frequency at a higher price;
• FERRY — throughput over comfort; CRUISE — comfort/experience as the product itself.

Safety is LOWEST in ROAD transport (and air), precisely where the largest passenger traffic occurs. Rail and maritime are comparatively safe.

Indicative EU data:
• ROAD: deaths fell ~67→51 per million inhabitants (2010–2019), ~42 in 2020 (COVID), ~46 in 2022;
• RAIL: 805 deaths in EU in 2022 (~1.8 per million);
• AIR: ~122 killed in 2023 (EU/EU-registered);
• MARITIME: very low (24–101 deaths/yr 2012–2022).

• greenhouse-gas (CO2) emissions & air pollution;
• NOISE pollution;
• WATER pollution (e.g. tyre microplastics entering waters & the food chain);
• biodiversity/habitat loss & land use;
• congestion (with its own emissions & time costs).
These 'external costs' are borne by society, not (fully) by the transport user.

• CO2 standards for new cars/vans → ZERO CO2 for new cars by 2035 (interim −55% by 2030); effective ban on new petrol/diesel car sales from 2035;
• Emissions Trading System (ETS) extended to road transport & buildings, plus maritime; aviation ETS revised;
• higher share of renewable transport fuels;
• removal of tax advantages for fossil fuels;
• including shipping (~8% of EU emissions) & aviation in carbon pricing.

1. Make ALL transport modes more SUSTAINABLE — low/zero-emission vehicles + renewable/low-carbon fuels;
2. Make sustainable ALTERNATIVES widely available in a seamless MULTIMODAL system — shift passengers to rail & public/active transport, freight to rail, inland waterways & short-sea shipping;
3. Put in place the right INCENTIVES — carbon pricing, taxation and infrastructure charging ('polluter pays' / 'user pays').

Smart mobility = seamless, safe, efficient connectivity driven by digitalisation & automation:
• connected & automated mobility (CCAM, autonomous vehicles);
• integrated multimodal information, booking & ticketing (Mobility as a Service);
• AI, 5G, a Common European Mobility Data Space;
• traffic-management systems (ERTMS for rail, Single European Sky for air);
• drones (unmanned aircraft) for commercial use.
